{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310553,"nonce":"CNvtCKfgOOP5a4nQjuVaai+qbKY=","title":"TCC getSymbol returns 64-bit pointer but MIR truncates to 32 bits, causing SIGSEGV when calling JIT code","message":"CC.call0 crashes on ARM64 macOS because `cc.getSymbol()` returns a 64-bit address (e.g., 0x1049c8000) but it gets truncated to 32 bits (0x049c8000) somewhere in the MIR pipeline.\n\n**Why:** The stdlib mapping for `getSymbol` specifies `return_type: Some(I64)` but the MIR wrapper or register allocation may treat the result as I32.\n\n**How to apply:** When investigating CC.call0 crashes, check the MIR wrapper generation for `rayzor_tcc_get_symbol` — specifically whether the return register is typed as I64 or I32. Also check the cranelift backend's handling of the call return value.\n\n**Also fixed:** TCC's `rt_mem` on macOS ARM64 now uses `mmap(MAP_JIT)` instead of `malloc` for JIT code memory (malloc memory can't be made executable on hardened runtime). The `protect_pages` function is no-op'd since MAP_JIT uses `pthread_jit_write_protect_np` instead of `mprotect`.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_cc_call0` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}